# Build output
bin/
obj/
artifacts/
publish/
*.user
*.suo

# Rider / Visual Studio / VS Code
.vs/
.idea/
.vscode/

# macOS
.DS_Store

# NuGet
*.nupkg
packages/

# Local runtime data produced during manual testing
logs/
scratch/

# Verification scratch output
/tmp-verify/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing material. NEVER commit any of this.
#
# A code signing certificate's private key identifies you as the publisher.
# Anyone holding it can sign software in your name. Keep it in a password
# manager or an offline backup, never in source control.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12
*.snk

# Exported code signing certificates. The public half (.cer) is safe to share
# but is generated per machine, so it is not tracked either.
*.cer
code-signing-backup.pfx

# The password that opens one of the above. A certificate and its password in the
# same place is a certificate with no password at all.
#
# These names are here because they are the ones the build looks for in
# ~/.pdftodicom, and a name a document tells somebody to use is a name that will
# eventually be typed inside a repository. *.p12 above is here for the same
# reason: it was missing while the documentation named it, and a certificate was
# committed under it.
codesign.password
*.password
password.txt
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Published executables.
#
# Each is around 70 MB, three are produced per release, and git keeps every
# one of them for ever. Committing them was growing this repository faster
# than it was worth, so they are no longer tracked.
#
# The pattern is deliberately general rather than one line per version: a
# release that adds its own three lines is a release that forgets to, and the
# cost of forgetting is 220 MB that cannot be taken out again without
# rewriting history.
#
# Where to get a build instead: run the Windows workflow by hand, or push a
# version tag, and the standalone executable, the handover package and the
# installer are attached to that run for all three architectures. Any commit
# can also be built locally with BUILD.bat or build/build.ps1.
#
# The READMEs under dist/ are tracked; the binaries beside them are not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
dist/**/*.exe
